#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "e03fa018-0d27-5038-8c77-cf6bb723f9cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5676" :question/problem "Suppose a teacher needs to select a group of $4$ students from a class of $20$ students to represent the class in a competition. How many different ways can the teacher form a team of $4$ students?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ students, so $n=20.$ We want to select $4$ of those students to form a group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways in which the teacher can form the team is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-4)!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{16!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{\\cancel{16!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{116\\,280}{24} \\\\  & =4\\,845.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "e87f2616-514d-5b27-9f41-055c1f32f0cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5677" :question/problem "There are $10$ employees in a company, and $3$ of them are to be selected to form a committee. How many different selections are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ employees, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those employees to form a committee, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to form the committee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{n}{C}_{r} & ={}_{10}{C}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{720}{6} \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320acdc4-d3ca-5bc8-85de-cea9cc2fa9c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations"}] :question/id #uuid "2c61e1e6-58d0-56dd-a63d-29bd5239add1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5723" :question/problem "Evaluate ${}_{7}C_5.$" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n                            {}_{7}C_5 &= \\dfrac {7!} {(7 - 5)!5!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {7!} {2!5!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\cancel{5!}} {2!\\cancel{5!}} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {7 \\cdot 6 } {2 \\cdot 1 } \\[5pt]\n \t\t\t    & = \\dfrac {42}{2} \\[5pt]\n                            &= 21.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "bafe3204-ab4c-5db4-ba21-b979e3090cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7458" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned})$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $\\displaystyle{{12}\\choose{10}}$ means the same thing as $_{12}C_{10}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n                            _{12}C_{10} &= \\dfrac {12!} {(12 - 10)!10!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {12!} {2!10!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {12 \\cdot 11 \\cdot \\cancel{10!}} {2!\\cdot\\cancel{10!}} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {12 \\cdot 11} {2} \\[5pt]\n \t                            &= 66.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec50a16-bb6b-5f05-b165-80436402a0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a47123d-f4ec-5210-b67f-61a4869b33fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254046} {:answer/id #uuid "3b29dafa-efbf-5a37-8d32-4a83de611c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254185} {:answer/id #uuid "82e767a8-42f1-58ae-87ff-b6da314b6877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254834} {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ca1418-3eca-579f-bc5d-83202ced8211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d62b2335-132d-58f4-9c9e-53b886749092" :question/math-academy-id "q-122550" :question/problem "In a group of six dancers, if every possible pair dances together, what is the total number of dances?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ people, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those people for each dance, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of dances is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194d45cd-0660-542c-94a3-1b5c301a67bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253883} {:answer/id #uuid "1e79ccb1-23d9-5225-8829-a064af6df4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253928} {:answer/id #uuid "3c683091-d481-544a-83d2-8667f5a82e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254194} {:answer/id #uuid "51d3db28-e0c2-5711-b2a2-64e7f338c36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254380} {:answer/id #uuid "d139fe8a-9303-57b5-9939-a4256f71032b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c683091-d481-544a-83d2-8667f5a82e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254194} :answer-field/id #uuid "902ecc83-43a2-5b9c-823c-165578b8e849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b44826df-0ce3-5fec-927d-76f3d23b53fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-122557" :question/problem "Consider the list of decimal digits from zero to nine that are multiples of three. If the order of the digits is not important, in how many ways can we choose two different digits from the list?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $4$ digits that are multiples of three ($0,3,6,9$), so $n=4.$ We want to select $2$ of those digits, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{4}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{4!}{(4-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{2!\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cec59a22-5d6d-5de8-9f8a-45ef6bb808a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,957\\,920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389820} {:answer/id #uuid "afeaf0f3-14db-58e7-8c72-651f40583589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,581\\,580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389821} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb700f8-9524-59c1-89ab-917235da5475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,209\\,112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389822} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc04816-e879-5d68-b950-f374a65870ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,662\\,100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389823} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b7429-84d6-539e-b5c5-1835b7badc17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afeaf0f3-14db-58e7-8c72-651f40583589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,581\\,580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389821} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7c9200-9af1-5711-bd23-c6a6abc5a745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bfaea9e-ab13-55ff-a72c-f7e351269ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1781" :question/problem "How many different ways can a group of four be made from a room of eighty people?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $80$ people, so $n=80.$ We want to select $4$ of those people for each group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of four is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{80}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{80!}{(80-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80!}{76!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77\\cdot \\cancel{76!}}{\\cancel{76!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{37\\,957\\,920}{24} \\\\  & =1\\,581\\,580.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320acdc4-d3ca-5bc8-85de-cea9cc2fa9c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0465a9a2-81b5-51e3-ad42-954e1c720351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281727} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7823d7-66d3-5ddf-a3fa-9a2abac0834d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282325} {:answer/id #uuid "6898f285-b852-50fe-8c84-769d39babe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282584} {:answer/id #uuid "8296b363-1fbc-56e7-b605-f8d4692f6d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282815} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb3f7a0-95c5-5575-b306-427aa0bba877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6898f285-b852-50fe-8c84-769d39babe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282584} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc25c905-259a-5a21-9caa-b20734813364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "895c8ec1-c196-5bfa-8399-032e5675637f" :question/math-academy-id "q-1799" :question/problem "Evaluate ${}_{6}{C}_{4}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ and $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{2!\\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed6c611-7080-523d-b896-c56dec755410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330362} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff9c65-84be-53b2-a929-b94803d25ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "604\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330431} {:answer/id #uuid "37d6cf36-8748-51b4-8b54-6228cec484b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330447} {:answer/id #uuid "969b6e1b-5091-543c-8c93-d0a9eaaed586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331287} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd6c370-23c0-504c-ba04-1d462eb14d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969b6e1b-5091-543c-8c93-d0a9eaaed586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331287} :answer-field/id #uuid "f589cd09-cb74-5eb6-8629-0dce6a7f1a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7767ffda-5aee-570f-a1e5-c14da7116c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-5569" :question/problem "Out of the ten contestants in an Olympic event, how many possible groups of $3$ medalists are there, regardless of which medal they win?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ contestants, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those contestants for the medal positions, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of medalists is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{720}{6} \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ffb173-ee4c-5e09-a585-dd1e58fa94f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351760} {:answer/id #uuid "3239606b-44a5-517f-b768-c29e460fbe6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352031} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8b10cf-17c7-5e4b-aff4-b9efbe784a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,568" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352899} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3c2dec-625c-574d-8f51-e85656efdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353010} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d79f9b-6aa0-52c1-9a8f-c395f4283907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15ffb173-ee4c-5e09-a585-dd1e58fa94f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351760} :answer-field/id #uuid "236f1413-e4be-5313-a884-4cdc94dd1f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14b01419-a43a-5786-a508-3298f924302b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85041" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{36}{33})$ means the same thing as ${}_{36}{C}_{33}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=36$ and $r=33,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{36}{C}_{33} & =\\frac{36!}{(36-33)!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36!}{3!33!} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34\\cdot \\cancel{33!}}{3!\\cdot \\cancel{33!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36\\cdot 35\\cdot 34}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42\\,840}{6} \\\\  & =7\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
